Return to the prompt after an error in PROMPT_COMMAND.
An interactive shell runs PROMPT_COMMAND under its own exception
handler, which printed ^C after an interrupt but called exitshell() for
any other exception. So a syntax error or a failed expansion in the
commands, such as ${x?} or an error in a function they call, ended the
interactive shell, although the same error in a command typed at the
prompt only returns to the prompt. The handler also recognized an
interrupt by bb_got_signal being SIGINT, but that stays set after any
Ctrl-C, such as one that stopped the previous command, so after one an
error or exit in the commands was handled as an interrupt. And the
interrupt path left their redirections and local variables in place.

Decide by the exception type, and after an interrupt or an error do
what the main loop does for an interactive shell: undo the shell state
the commands left behind (the reset in exitreset(), pushed input files
and local variables), keep $? as it was, and go on to the prompt. Input
already read but not yet parsed is kept, since it does not belong to
the commands. exit and the other exceptions still end the shell.

---
 shell/ash.c |   11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -15799,8 +15799,15 @@
 					checkkwd = 0;
 					heredoclist = 0;
 					evalstring((char *)cmd, 0);
-				} else if (bb_got_signal == SIGINT) {
-					write(STDOUT_FILENO, "^C\n", 3);
+				} else if (exception_type == EXINT
+						|| exception_type == EXERROR) {
+					/* Clean up as after an error in a command,
+					 * but keep any input not yet read. */
+					if (exception_type == EXINT)
+						write(STDOUT_FILENO, "^C\n", 3);
+					exitreset();
+					popallfiles();
+					unwindlocalvars(NULL);
 				} else {
 					exitreset();
 					exitshell();
